    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_8ecf145017b32d502634d185e14f6c290b08ff1e1422a1b28c90f5f6bbcb92f1",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Root-discriminant invariance links two opposite uses of degree growth: for a particular field it lets discriminant lower bounds exclude a nontrivial Hilbert class field and constrain the class number, whereas infinite class-field towers turn repeated degree growth at bounded root discriminant into a structural counterexample to global divergence and motivate separating the restricted degree patterns reached by tower constructions from prime degree.",
